add("abvm-451e9a28177ccc30755e","reading","Reading","theme","Why is “Friends can solve problems by working together” a theme instead of a story detail?",{"It tells exactly where the story happens.","It names one object in the story.","It is a broad lesson that can apply to many situations."},3,"Themes are broader messages, not single facts.","Ask whether the idea could apply to another story.",3,"current","auto-theme-it-is-a-broad-lesson-that-can-apply-to-many-situations-e317f9")
add("abvm-702be93a604fc14fee49","reading","Reading","theme","A character admits a mistake and fixes what was damaged. Which message is the strongest theme?",{"Taking responsibility helps repair mistakes.","Damaged things are always useless.","Mistakes should always be hidden."},1,"The character owns the mistake and works to make it right.","Focus on the lesson from the character’s choice.",3,"current","auto-theme-taking-responsibility-helps-repair-mistakes-7f93b2")
add("abvm-cf66f236bf8b560078af","reading","Reading","visualize","Read: “Tiny raindrops tapped the window while gray clouds covered the sky.” Which picture best matches?",{"A bright beach with large waves","A gray rainy scene outside a window","A sunny playground with no clouds"},2,"The words raindrops, window, and gray clouds create that mental picture.","Match the picture to the exact describing words.",2,"current","auto-visualize-a-gray-rainy-scene-outside-a-window-525c8d")
add("abvm-588c19daf98ffa0cbbfe","reading","Reading","visualize","Which detail is most important for visualizing “golden leaves covered the path like a crunchy blanket”?",{"Someone may walk later.","The sentence has many words.","The path is covered with golden leaves."},3,"Color and where the leaves are located are concrete details that build the mental picture.","Choose a detail you could actually picture in your mind.",3,"current","auto-visualize-the-path-is-covered-with-golden-leaves-431aeb")
add("abvm-2cb4d2742fe95ca7c5be","reading","Reading","visualize","Read: “A bright red kite dipped and climbed in the windy blue sky.” What should you picture?",{"A red kite moving in a windy blue sky","A dark cave with a lantern","A snowy road at night"},1,"The sentence gives color, movement, wind, and sky details.","Use only details the sentence provides.",2,"current","auto-visualize-a-red-kite-moving-in-a-windy-blue-sky-19c580")
add("abvm-4bd90e6f469ece3a0135","reading","Reading","visualize","Read: “Steam curled above the warm soup while rain tapped the kitchen window.” Which picture matches?",{"A dry sandbox in bright sun","Warm soup by a rainy window","A frozen lake under stars"},2,"Steam, soup, rain, and the window create that mental picture.","Match several details at once.",2,"current","auto-visualize-warm-soup-by-a-rainy-window-857cd6")
add("abvm-9bb85b1d8e37a815b5f7","reading","Reading","visualize","Read: “The tiny puppy curled into the soft blue blanket beside the couch.” What should you visualize?",{"A horse running across a field","A bird sitting on a roof","A small puppy resting in a blue blanket"},3,"The details describe the puppy, blanket, and location.","Picture the specific nouns and describing words.",2,"current","auto-visualize-a-small-puppy-resting-in-a-blue-blanket-8b2f53")
add("abvm-963d9cd83a85a6c29972","reading","Reading","visualize","Which words do the most work in helping you visualize “silver moonlight shimmered across the quiet pond”?",{"silver, shimmered, quiet pond","the, across, the","moonlight is a word"},1,"Those concrete describing words give color, movement, and setting.","Choose words that create a sensory picture.",3,"current","auto-visualize-silver-shimmered-quiet-pond-e89578")
add("abvm-90ea67734a7ed8679a92","reading","Reading","visualize","Why does “crunchy brown leaves covered the sidewalk” create a clearer picture than “things were outside”?",{"It has more letters.","It gives specific sensory and location details.","It uses the word sidewalk."},2,"Specific details make the mental image clearer.","Compare how much you can actually picture.",3,"current","auto-visualize-it-gives-specific-sensory-and-location-details-488b64")
add("abvm-182c171d1eba10865f69","reading","Reading","dialogue","Which part of this sentence is dialogue: “I found my book!” Lena shouted.",{"Lena shouted.","my book","“I found my book!”"},3,"Dialogue is the exact speech of a character and is shown inside quotation marks.","Look for the words the character actually says.",2,"current","auto-dialogue-i-found-my-book-1db336")
add("abvm-d391ae2318940a221243","reading","Reading","dialogue","Why do quotation marks help a reader recognize dialogue?",{"They show the exact words a character says.","They show where the story takes place.","They tell how many paragraphs are in a story."},1,"Quotation marks separate a character’s spoken words from the narration around them.","Think about what information quotation marks surround.",3,"current","auto-dialogue-they-show-the-exact-words-a-character-says-6fe19d")
add("abvm-4391acc71845fe5df9d9","reading","Reading","dialogue","Which words are dialogue in: “I can help,” Noah said.",{"Noah said.","“I can help,”","help Noah"},2,"The quotation marks show Noah’s exact spoken words.","Find the words the character actually says.",2,"current","auto-dialogue-i-can-help-b8321e")
add("abvm-5d2a94d4cd1eeec42c51","reading","Reading","dialogue","Read: Ava whispered, “The baby is sleeping.” What did Ava say?",{"Ava whispered.","The narrator said Ava was loud.","“The baby is sleeping.”"},3,"The words inside quotation marks are Ava’s dialogue.","Look inside the quotation marks.",2,"current","auto-dialogue-the-baby-is-sleeping-3a44a5")
add("abvm-1e572fadf15e2639810e","reading","Reading","dialogue","Which sentence uses dialogue correctly?",{"“Let’s go outside,” Ben said.","Let’s go outside, Ben said.","Ben said lets “go outside”."},1,"The character’s exact words are placed inside quotation marks.","Find the sentence that clearly marks spoken words.",2,"current","auto-dialogue-let-s-go-outside-ben-said-dca232")
add("abvm-7a60bb36f18448c67559","reading","Reading","dialogue","In “Wait for me!” Mia called, what do the quotation marks tell the reader?",{"Mia is the narrator.","Those are Mia’s exact spoken words.","The story happens outside."},2,"Quotation marks separate dialogue from narration.","Think about what quotation marks surround.",3,"current","auto-dialogue-those-are-mia-s-exact-spoken-words-01300a")
add("abvm-8405caf5616cb906d500","reading","Reading","dialogue","Why is “Dad said, ‘Dinner is ready.’” partly dialogue and partly narration?",{"Every word is dialogue.","None of the words are spoken.","The quoted words are spoken; “Dad said” tells who spoke."},3,"Dialogue gives exact speech while narration identifies the speaker.","Separate the quoted words from the speech tag.",3,"current","auto-dialogue-the-quoted-words-are-spoken-dad-said-tells-who-spoke-813305")
add("abvm-baa5ab4579c9be7338b8","reading","Reading","main-character","A story follows Ben as he trains for a race, worries before the start, and celebrates at the finish. Who is the main character?",{"Ben","the race","the finish line"},1,"The story follows Ben’s actions and feelings across the whole sequence.","Choose the person the story follows most closely.",2,"current","auto-main-character-ben-34a10c")
add("abvm-9d8da382dbbf04070289","reading","Reading","main-character","Why is Ben the main character in a story that follows his problem, choices, and result?",{"His name has three letters.","Most of the important events happen to or because of Ben.","He appears only in the title."},2,"The main character is central to the important events and changes in the story.","Think about whose actions drive the story.",3,"current","auto-main-character-most-of-the-important-events-happen-to-or-because-of-ben-61094e")
add("abvm-ddf8d1f1ad7ec276097f","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","In the sentence “The birds sing in the tree,” which words are the predicate?",{"The birds","the tree","sing in the tree"},3,"The predicate tells what the subject does: the birds sing in the tree.","First find the subject, then choose the words that tell what it does.",2,"current","auto-subject-predicate-sing-in-the-tree-741dd3")
add("abvm-6487a9845091904799f2","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","Which explanation correctly separates the subject and predicate in “Mia opened the window”?",{"Mia is the subject; opened the window is the predicate.","Opened is the subject; Mia is the predicate.","The window is the subject; Mia opened is the predicate."},1,"Mia names who the sentence is about, and opened the window tells what Mia did.","The subject names who or what; the predicate tells what that subject does or is.",3,"current","auto-subject-predicate-mia-is-the-subject-opened-the-window-is-the-predicate-28f606")
add("abvm-d7a859017d14e5c8408b","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","In “My little brother builds a tower,” which words are the subject?",{"builds a tower","My little brother","a tower"},2,"The subject tells who the sentence is about.","Ask who is doing the action.",2,"current","auto-subject-predicate-my-little-brother-2af298")
add("abvm-75f860bbd0dbedb4a77f","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","In “The yellow bus stopped by the school,” which words are the predicate?",{"The yellow bus","the school","stopped by the school"},3,"The predicate tells what the yellow bus did.","Find the action and the words that go with it.",2,"current","auto-subject-predicate-stopped-by-the-school-d4895a")
add("abvm-d368d377fcaa56d79fe1","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","Which pair correctly labels the parts of “The rabbit hopped across the yard”?",{"subject: The rabbit; predicate: hopped across the yard","subject: hopped; predicate: The rabbit","subject: the yard; predicate: The rabbit hopped"},1,"The rabbit names who the sentence is about; hopped across the yard tells what it did.","Split the sentence into who or what, then what that subject does.",2,"current","auto-subject-predicate-subject-the-rabbit-predicate-hopped-across-the-yard-18d487")
add("abvm-c4c8d21d0618dbc360ee","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","Why is “The tall tree” the subject in “The tall tree swayed in the wind”?",{"It tells what the tree did.","It names what the sentence is about.","It names where the action happened."},2,"A subject names who or what the sentence is about.","Decide whether the words name the subject or tell what it does.",3,"current","auto-subject-predicate-it-names-what-the-sentence-is-about-d28466")
add("abvm-37433355e58f6c23335a","grammar","Grammar","subject-predicate","Which sentence is correctly split into subject and predicate?",{"Read a | new book our class.","A new book | our class read.","Our class | read a new book."},3,"Our class is the complete subject, and read a new book is the predicate.","Keep all the subject words together, then all the words telling what the subject does.",3,"current","auto-subject-predicate-our-class-read-a-new-book-df7483")
add("abvm-07574cb3c12a21e866fe","religion","Religion","religion-trinity","According to the current Religion lesson, a note says “one God in three Persons: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.” Which lesson topic does that statement describe?",{"the Trinity","being made in God's image and likeness","caring for God's creation"},1,"The current lesson uses one God in three Persons — Father, Son, and Holy Spirit — to explain the Trinity.","Match the three Persons named in the statement to the lesson topic.",2,"current","auto-religion-trinity-the-trinity-7cc1ac")
